WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.560
A contractor is attempting to mitigate moisture intrusion from the soil into the proposed

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.080
building's subsurface wall.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:13.519
It is also desired to mitigate additional stresses on the building.

00:00:13.519 --> 00:00:18.679
The contractor has access to various materials at a local baro pit, with which they can

00:00:18.679 --> 00:00:22.960
construct a barrier between the soil and the wall.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:28.400
According to the Unified Soil Classification System, which of the following options below

00:00:28.399 --> 00:00:31.799
is most suitable for their goals?

00:00:31.799 --> 00:00:34.280
So this is another concept problem.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:40.320
The goal is to use the USCIS soil classification chart to decide which of our options will

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:47.480
be best in terms of two goals, mitigating both moisture intrusion and stresses upon the

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:48.959
structure.

00:00:48.959 --> 00:00:52.480
So go to that table and let's go through our options.

00:00:53.119 --> 00:00:59.319
First, the chart tells us that SW is a well graded sand.

00:00:59.319 --> 00:01:05.640
This is usually used in drainage, so would serve the opposite purpose of our goal of mitigating

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.640
moisture intrusion.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:12.799
This sand would allow moisture to easily pass through it, which is a good thing if our stated

00:01:12.799 --> 00:01:17.400
goal was to drain moisture rather than prevent its intrusion.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:21.439
It can also be more stable than other soils.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:25.560
The next three options are all clays.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:31.120
Clays are often used in waterproofing efforts, so we need to narrow down the most appropriate.

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:37.799
We can choose between a lean clay, a fat clay, and an organic clay.

00:01:37.799 --> 00:01:42.359
We can remove the organic clay since that group symbol is also shared with organic

00:01:42.359 --> 00:01:43.680
silt.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:49.640
Aside from this variability not being useful when selecting a building material, usually soils

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:55.799
containing large amounts of organics are not desirable in a building material.

00:01:55.799 --> 00:01:59.120
So that leaves us with lean clay and fat clay.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:00.879
Both are inorganic.

00:02:00.879 --> 00:02:06.159
However, fat clay has a higher liquid limit, meaning that it will be more susceptible to

00:02:06.159 --> 00:02:08.280
deformation.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:13.240
Since one of the goals is to mitigate additional stresses on the building, that would remove

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:15.960
this option as well.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:21.480
This leaves us with a lean clay as the most appropriate option from the list given.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:26.180
Lean clays do not swell as much as fat clays when subjected to water, and are still

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:30.200
useful in terms of mitigating moisture migration.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:34.960
So this conceptual problem can be solved by using a general knowledge of soils and their

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:40.000
use as well as the information given in the USCS chart.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:40.439
And that's it.
